fine-structure-constant.conceptual_definition:
The fine-structure constant, denoted by the Greek letter alpha (\( 
alpha 
)), is a fundamental dimensionless physical constant that characterizes the strength of the electromagnetic interaction. It represents the coupling strength between electrons and photons and is crucial in quantum electrodynamics (QED). It is a pure number, meaning it is independent of the units used to measure the physical quantities involved.
